<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burchard of Worms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 965–<a href="/years/1025">1025</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burchard of Worms was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Worms from 1000 to 1025. Between about 1008 and 1012 he compiled the Decretum, a collection of canon law in twenty books that became the standard handbook of church law until Gratian in the 12th century. He also issued a law code for the dependants of his church and strengthened the bishop's authority over the city of Worms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burchard-of-worms.jpg" alt="Burchard of Worms" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heidas Wikipedia account All pictures Please use this discussion page · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Worms_Doom_2005-05-27b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Burchard came from a wealthy, well-connected family of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>; his older brother Franco was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Worms before him, and his sister Mathilda became an abbess near Worms. As a boy he entered the monastic school of St. Florin or St. Kastor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. Under Archbishop Willigis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> he rose through the clerical grades and became provost of St. Victor in Mainz, where he founded a monastery with a cloister of canons that Willigis funded. When Franco died in 999, Emperor Otto III appointed Burchard to Worms in 1000, after the men he had first intended for it died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His biographer describes Worms as in disrepair, troubled by robbers and wolves. Burchard rebuilt the city walls, founded monasteries and churches, and in a violent quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, whom he accused of sheltering criminals, won the aid of Emperor Henry II; the duke's castle was dismantled and rebuilt as a monastery of Paul of Tarsus. He took in a child of the duke's family who became Emperor Conrad II. In 1016 he rebuilt the Cathedral of St. Peter, and he taught in its school. He died on 20 August 1025, leaving his sister a hair shirt and an iron chain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Burchard compiled the Decretum with Bishop Walter of Speyer, Alpert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and other clergy, drawing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a>, Gregory the Great, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrabanus-maurus">Hrabanus Maurus</a> and Julian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. Over 77 complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survive, the earliest made at Worms under his supervision. Book 19, the Corrector, is a confessor's guide that is probably a 10th-century work he added. The Decretum was later supplanted by the Panormia of Ivo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> and the Decretum of Gratian. From 1023 to 1025 he issued the Lex Familiae, 31 chapters of customary law for the free and unfree workers of the episcopal estate, on marriage, abduction, murder, theft and perjury.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burchard of Worms” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bishop <b>Burchard of Worms</b>, who issued a famous collection of canon law around 1020, gained wide rights over the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-worms">Prince-Bishopric of Worms</a></li></ul></section>
